Let an unattended judging say where it is (P-286)

Found in the field during the first production judging on the P-280
build: three hours in, the run log said Phase=EvaluatingObjects and
nothing else, and the owner was reduced to watching a file's size change
to decide whether to wait or abandon.

That is the state P-278 was opened to end, and it ended only half of it.
The phases reached the log. The object count never did, because Judge in
the command line passed JudgingRun.NobodyIsWatching and every progress
report the judging raised was thrown away.

The reasoning recorded beside that call was:

    "a scheduled run has no operator to tell, and a log filling with
     counts nobody reads is what the predecessor's progress reporting
     became."

The second half is right, and is why this is rate limited rather than
simply switched on. The first half is exactly backwards. An unattended
run is precisely the one nobody can watch, so the log is the only
witness it has - and the judging that most needs to say where it is is
the one running overnight against nine domains.

JudgingHeartbeat writes a "still judging" line carrying the phase, the
running total, how many objects moved since the last line, and a rate in
objects per minute. The delta and the rate are the point: a stalled run
reports zero, where a bare total would only reveal a stall by being
compared against a line that scrolled off an hour ago.

RATE LIMITED BY ELAPSED TIME, NOT BY OBJECT COUNT

A user with four permissions and a container with four thousand are both
one object. A line every N objects is therefore minutes apart in one
part of a forest and hours apart in another - and it is elapsed time an
operator is judging when deciding whether to keep waiting. Five minutes
is roughly twenty lines across an eight-hour run: enough to answer the
question, few enough to leave the log readable.

AccountingEvery WAS ALSO WRONG, AND SAID SO ITSELF

Its remark admitted the figure was "not tuned against a real forest,
because no measurement of one exists yet." One exists now. The
production run was committing roughly 500 objects every fourteen
minutes, which puts 100,000 objects nearly two days apart rather than
the "nine or ten accountings across a run measured in hours" the comment
assumed - and the run reached hour three having written no accounting at
all. Now 10,000, which gives that run one roughly every four hours and a
laboratory capture none, which is correct: a capture finishing in twenty
seconds has its full accounting at the end.

ProgressEvery stays at 1,000. Its remark now says what it is - an offer
rate, with rate limiting left to whoever is listening.

Four checks, and both halves mutation-tested with explicit rebuilds:

  - disabling the rate limit fails ReportsInsideOneIntervalAreNotWritten
    and ARunCrossingThreeIntervalsSaysSoThreeTimes
  - reporting the running total as the delta fails
    AStalledRunReportsNoObjectsSinceTheLastLine

RunLogFile already takes a lock on every write, so progress arriving
from the judging thread needed no new synchronisation - checked rather
than assumed.

3,450 Judgement, Cli, Shell and EndToEnd tests green.

Does not affect the run in flight, which was started from the earlier
build.
